## 4. Defects from the follow-up round (all fixed)

Removing the blanket `#![allow(clippy::all, dead_code)]` from the agent was the
single most productive change: its dead-code warnings were pointing at three
unimplemented features.

| # | Sev | Defect | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 13 | P1 | Agent `ListBackups` returned an empty list, and `backup::list` deserialised restic's `snapshots --json` into a struct whose fields do not exist in that output | proper `ResticSnapshot` DTO mapping `id`/`time`/`tags`/`summary` into panel backups, wired into dispatch |
| 14 | P1 | "Sync from node" sent an **empty** restic target, ignored the reply, and always flashed success | resolves the destination, upserts rows (idempotent on `site_id + snapshot_id`), audit-logs, reports real counts or the error |
| 15 | P1 | Import was impossible: the agent looked the site up in its store first and always answered *"site N is not managed by this agent"* | `Operation::ImportSite` carries an `ImportRequest` with the site definition; the agent builds the record on a first import, resync still requires one |
| 16 | P1 | Import's search/replace was passed the **database name** as the search string, so it replaced nothing | reads the real old URL with `wp option get siteurl`, replaces it with `https://<domain>`, or notes "site URL unchanged" |
| 17 | P1 | Clone/staging copied the database with an empty MySQL password | new `ops/wpconfig.rs` parses the source `wp-config.php` (textually, never executing PHP) and the copy uses those credentials |
| 18 | P1 | `wordpress::set_db_config` passed `--raw` for `DB_NAME`/`DB_USER`/`DB_PASSWORD`, writing unquoted PHP — a parse error in `wp-config.php` | `--raw` removed |
| 19 | P1 | A viewer POSTing a malformed form got `422` from the extractor, never reaching the role check | read-only enforcement moved into `require_session` middleware (`db::teams::can_mutate`), audit-logged, applies before any extractor |
| 20 | P2 | Import carried the SSH password through hidden form fields **and** stored SSH + remote-DB passwords in `jobs.payload` in plaintext | new `credentials::CredentialStash` (in-memory, user-scoped, 15-min TTL, one-shot) for the wizard; payload sealed with `SecretBox` |
| 21 | P2 | Log grep silently stripped "metacharacters" from the pattern, so searching for `$_GET[` searched for something else; missing log file surfaced a raw `tail:` error; no-match exited 1 as an error | pattern and path shell-quoted, `grep -F` handles literals, `|| true` for no-match, missing file is an empty state, rendered through a template so Askama escapes log lines |
| 22 | P2 | `db::sites::backups()` labelled every worker- or sync-recorded backup `local` | falls back to `restic_repo` before `local` |
| 23 | P2 | `ResticTarget` assembled inline at four call sites, so `repo_prefix` was never used | single `db::destinations::restic_target` + `for_site`, with tests |
| 24 | P2 | `apply_effects` fabricated a 1.15 GB backup size when a job ran the simulated path | records `snapshot_id = "simulated"`, 0 bytes; `files_bytes`/`db_bytes` now flow through the protocol |

Hardening done along the way, not tied to a specific defect:

- `exec::shell_quote` (POSIX single-quote escaping), verified by a test that
  round-trips eight hostile values through a real `sh -c printf`. Applied to the
  clone database pipeline, the log grep, and the **remote** `mysqldump` the
  import runs over SSH, which had interpolated host/user/name unquoted into a
  command executed by the remote shell.
- Notification insert/resolve SQL moved out of `alerts.rs` into
  `db::notifications` so it can be tested.

